{"name":"Bugle Call","hex":"#d3973c","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bugle","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A horn used by hunters.","A simple brass instrument consisting of a horn with no valves, playing only pitches in its harmonic series"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To announce, sing, or cry in the manner of a musical bugle."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bugle"},{"term":"call","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To reach out with one's voice.\n To request, summon, or beckon.","To request, summon, or beckon."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cry or shout.","The characteristic cry of a bird or other animal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/call"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bugle call","blocks":[{"text":"A bugle call is a short tune, originating as a military signal announcing scheduled and certain non-scheduled events on a military installation, battlefield, or ship. Historically, bugles, drums, and other loud musical instruments were used for clear communication in the noise and confusion of a battlefield. Naval bugle calls were also used to command the crew of many warships."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c5/Bugler_of_the_French_Marine_Infantry%2C_Kuwait_City%2C_1991.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bugle_call","wikidata":"Q1547148"},{"title":"The Bugle Call","blocks":[{"text":"The Bugle Call is a 1927 American silent drama film directed by Edward Sedgwick and starring Jackie Coogan and Claire Windsor, which was released on August 6, 1927."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Bugle_Call_lobby_card.jpg/330px-Bugle_Call_lobby_card.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Bugle_Call","wikidata":"Q7720524"},{"title":"Bugle","blocks":[{"text":"The bugle is a simple signaling brass instrument with a wide conical bore. It normally has no valves or other pitch-altering devices, and is thus limited to its natural harmonic notes, and pitch is controlled entirely by varying the air and embouchure."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Bugle_in_C_MET_DP-12679-123.jpg/330px-Bugle_in_C_MET_DP-12679-123.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bugle","wikidata":"Q1628293"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Memorial_Stained_Glass_window%2C_Class_of_1934%2C_Royal_Military_College_of_Canada.jpg/250px-Memorial_Stained_Glass_window%2C_Class_of_1934%2C_Royal_Military_College_of_Canada.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/36/Memorial_Stained_Glass_window%2C_Class_of_1934%2C_Royal_Military_College_of_Canada.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Memorial_Stained_Glass_window%2C_Class_of_1934%2C_Royal_Military_College_of_Canada.jpg/250px-Memorial_Stained_Glass_window%2C_Class_of_1934%2C_Royal_Military_College_of_Canada.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Memorial Stained Glass window, Class of 1934, Royal Military College of Canada showing Officer Cadet playing the Bugle call for Last Post or The Rouse","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMemorial_Stained_Glass_window%2C_Class_of_1934%2C_Royal_Military_College_of_Canada.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Trumpetcallsa.jpg/500px-Trumpetcallsa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Trumpetcallsa.jpg/1280px-Trumpetcallsa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=par